n**Where the symmetry axes are.** The fact sheet shows that the mirror is off-centre. Row y mirrors to row 31−y, not 29−y, and the test shows the same for columns: x mirrors to 31−x. So rows 0–1 and columns 0–1 have no mirror partner inside the grid. The test input is also about 96% symmetric under the transpose, (y, x) ↔ (x, y). Combining these gives each cell up to 7 partners:\n- (y, 31−x), (31−y, x), (31−y, 31−x)\n- (x, y), (x, 31−y), (31−x, y), (31−x, 31−y)\n\n**Why the transpose partners come last.** In train pair 1 the transpose agreement was measured at only 76%, which looks weak. That figure was taken on a damaged grid, so it does not settle whether the transpose really holds. To stay safe, the program tries the pure mirror partners first. It uses transpose-based partners only when every mirror partner is outside the grid or is itself 0. A cell in rows 0–1 that is also in columns 0–1 has no mirror partner at all, so it needs the transpose.\n\n**Construction.**\n1. Find every 0 cell.\n2. For each one, take the value of the first partner that lies inside the grid and is non-zero, in the order listed above.\n3. Some cells may have only damaged partners on the first pass. So repeat the pass, using cells filled in earlier passes, until nothing is left to fill.\n4. After each pass, emit the canvas as one repair stage.\n\n**Applying it to the test.** The test has two 0 patches:\n- The block at rows 26–29, columns 25–29 is repaired from its mirror images across y = 15.5 and x = 15.5.\n- The strip at rows 12–16, columns 9–10 is repaired from its left-right mirror at columns 21–22, or from its up-down mirror.\n\n**How this was checked.** I have not run this program yet. On submission it will be checked cell by cell against all four train pairs. If the transpose partners turn out to be wrong in pair 1, that check will show the exact mismatches.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid).copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    N = 31  # mirror: a -> 31 - a\n\n    def partners(y, x):\n        # mirror partners first, transpose-based partners as a fallback\n        return [(y, N - x), (N - y, x), (N - y, N - x),\n                (x, y), (x, N - y), (N - x, y), (N - x, N - y)]\n\n    for _ in range(10):\n        holes = list(zip(*np.where(g == 0)))\n        if not holes:\n            break\n        changed = False\n        new = g.copy()\n        for (y, x) in holes:\n            for (py, px) in partners(y, x):\n                if 0 <= py < H and 0 <= px < W and g[py, px] != 0:\n                    new[y, x] = g[py, px]\n                    changed = True\n                    break\n        g = new\n        emit(g, \"Filled masked (colour 0) cells from their symmetric partners.\")\n        if not changed:\n            break\n    emit(g, \"Final repaired grid.\")\n    return g\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"This puzzle is a **symmetry repair** task."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the grids are made of.** Each input is a 30×30 mosaic with a strong mirror symmetry."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Where the symmetry axes are.** The fact sheet shows that the mirror is off-centre."}],"repair":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"That figure was taken on a damaged grid, so it does not settle whether the transpose really holds."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Some cells may have only damaged partners on the first pass."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Final repaired grid."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"89ee7d5d5792edccb00520abfe2dc647d2f330daf65985cd23bb98b34cdb2373","task_sha256":"507eacd7845133332f020879dea180bd80a62c83433051b4f78cdeba5e9e830f","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
